    "created_at": "2019-07-11T07:31:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-07-11T07:31:36Z",
    "body": "> Something like 2019-X7 is presumably invalid as the X must be 0?\r\n> \r\n> I guess one would have to buy the standard to be really sure? Should this affect JavaScript's `Date` object?\r\n\r\nAs far as my understanding goes, the `X` generally has to be in the right-most position, so `X7` would be invalid.\r\n\r\nAnother use case, where this could be useful is the situation if I have to state that I'm over 18 on a website - with this kind of validation, I could just provide a year without necessarily needing to disclose my full birth date. This might be good from a privacy  / data minimisation point of view.\r\n\r\nSince Forms and Input are often bound / handled through the JavaScript `Date` object, so support for fuzzy dates in the scripting language might be useful. On the other hand, the `Date.dateString` already defines a [simplified](http://www.ecma-international.org/ecma-262/5.1/#sec-15.9.1.15) version of the ISO8601 format, likely in order to reduce ambiguities.\r\n\r\nMy main issue was, that the 'input type date' validation prohibits the use of fuzzy dates explicitly, excluding people from entering their (undefined) birthdates. Any way to handle fuzzy dates might already be enough, for instance just setting specific parts of a date (M, D, Y) to be `undefined`, or by having a boolean flag to disable validation (user-triggered), as suggested by [StommePoes](https://github.com/w3c/html/issues/1340#issuecomment-399037728).",
